abstract: >-
  A composite number n whose totient divides n-1 was known to have at least
  fourteen prime factors. We prove that it has at least sixteen, at least
  16001 if (n-1)/phi(n) >= 3, and more than 10^8 if 3 divides n, and that
  n < 2^(2^(k-7)) if n has k prime factors. For the companion equation
  phi(n) | n+1 we show that the nine known solutions are the only ones with
  at most eight prime factors, and that a further solution prime to 3 has at
  least sixteen. Each statement is reduced by hand to a finite computation,
  which independent programs carry out. Lehmer's conjecture remains open.
